# {{ entity_singular | capitalize }} scout agent

Runs one bounded research pass per invocation, finds candidates you aren't
already tracking, and stages good matches in the tracker for you to review
and act on yourself. Every run is capped — see Budget below — so a
scheduled/repeated run can't run away on cost.

Sources this project is set up to use: **{{ scout_sources | join(', ') }}**. Every
enabled source is covered on every run. To change the list, edit
`scout_sources` in the project's config and regenerate, or edit the
sections below directly.

## Hard rules — read before doing anything

- **Never submit an application/form.** No filling out a submission form, no
  clicking Submit/Apply on any site, ever, for any reason.
- **Never create an account** on any external site.
- **Never enter personal data into a form** (name, address, phone, or any
  other personal identifiers) on any external site.
- The job this skill does ends at: *find → check whether {% if artifact_enabled %}{{ artifact_label | lower }} is needed{% else %}anything needs drafting{% endif %}
  → draft{% if artifact_enabled %} (via the {{ artifact_slug }} skill){% endif %} → write a {{ queued_stage | tojson }} row → summarize in
  chat*. Nothing external gets touched beyond reading public listings/postings.
- Only read sources that are publicly reachable. Don't log in anywhere or
  drive a browser session on the user's behalf.
{% if job_sources %}
- The helper tools in `{{ tools }}/` only make plain GET requests to public
  pages and APIs. Never modify them to send data, log in, or use anyone's
  credentials.
{% endif %}

## Budget (read this before searching anything)

These numbers exist specifically to keep token spend predictable. Do not
expand scope mid-run to "try to hit" a number — report honestly if a run
comes up short rather than searching more sources than planned. The preset
for this project is **{{ scout_budget_preset }}**; change the numbers in this
table directly if the defaults don't fit — they were a reasonable starting
guess, not a fixed rule.

| Parameter | Value |
|---|---|
| **Early-exit trigger** | As soon as **{{ scout_candidates_target }} A-tier candidates** (see Match tiers below) are found, stop searching — even if the planned sources/terms or the fetch cap hasn't been used up. Don't keep searching just to fill toward the hard cap — hitting the target in A-tier candidates is "done," not a floor. |
| Hard cap on candidates queued per run | **{{ scout_candidates_cap }}** — stop immediately on reaching this, mid-search if needed |
| Sources covered per run | All enabled: {{ scout_sources | join(', ') }} |
{% if uses_web %}
| Web-search terms per run | {{ scout_terms_per_run }} (of `search_terms.md`'s list — rotate through the rest on later runs if not "all") |
{% endif %}
| Full posting fetches per run (hard cap) | {{ scout_jd_fetch_cap }} — stop fetching more once hit, work with what's been reviewed. Listing results from the helper tools do NOT count; reading one posting's full text does. |
| Research subagents spawned | {{ scout_subagents_per_run }} per run. Only fan out to more parallel subagents for an explicit manual "deep sweep" you ask for directly — never as the default behavior |

### Match tiers

Used to know when the early-exit trigger fires. Keep this simple — a
one-line judgment call per candidate, not a scoring rubric:

- **A-tier ("strong")**: meets essentially all of `criteria.md` cleanly — no real gaps.
- **B-tier ("maybe")**: meets criteria with one real gap flagged (partial-but-real).
{% if scout_tier_mode == 'strong_only' %}
  **This project queues strong matches only.** Do not queue B-tier candidates:
  log each in `reviewed_postings.jsonl` as `rejected` with the reason
  `maybe (B-tier): <the gap>`, so a future run doesn't re-fetch it.
{% else %}
  Still worth queuing, just don't count it toward the early-exit trigger.
{% endif %}
{% if has_coaching %}
- **Their #1 comes first.** `criteria.md` ranks what matters most. An A-tier
  match has to fit their #1 as far as the posting shows; one that misses it is
  at best B-tier, and the rationale says so.
{% endif %}
- Don't bother tiering rejects — if it doesn't clear the bar to be at least
  B-tier, it's not a candidate, just note it in `reviewed_postings.jsonl`
  (below) and move on.
- **Never pad toward the target with a weak-fit candidate.** If the sources
  genuinely produce fewer real candidates than the target, queue what's real
  and say so plainly — don't reach into a borderline-fit candidate from a
  long-tail source just to round the count up.
- **Same-company dedup needs content, not just title, comparison.** When a
  new posting's title differs from an existing tracked row at the same
  company only by a level word (Senior/Staff/Lead) or minor wording, read
  both and compare the actual descriptions before treating it as genuinely
  different — a level-ladder pair is one role, not two.

{% if has_coaching %}
### Work authorization screen

Applies when `criteria.md`'s "Work authorization" section says they need
sponsorship or an E-Verify employer. Judge from what the posting says; never
state immigration rules or fees as fact in a note.

- **Skip** (log in `reviewed_postings.jsonl` as `rejected`, reason
  `work authorization: "<the posting's words>"`) when the posting says it
  won't sponsor, requires authorization to work without sponsorship now or in
  the future, requires US citizenship or a security clearance, or restricts
  export-controlled work to citizens or permanent residents.
- **Red flag, skip** (reason `red flag: asks the candidate to pay for
  sponsorship`) when the posting asks the candidate to pay sponsorship or legal
  costs.
- **Not stated**: still a candidate; end the notes line with "Sponsorship not
  stated; ask early."
- **Worth noting** in the notes line: the posting says it sponsors; the
  employer is a university or a nonprofit research organization ("may be
  exempt from the H-1B lottery; confirm"); for STEM OPT, whether E-Verify
  participation is stated.
- If their risk comfort is low, or a layoff would put their status on a clock,
  weigh stability when tiering: note company size or stage when the posting
  shows it.

{% endif %}
### Overflow backlog

When the {{ scout_candidates_cap }}-candidate cap is hit mid-run (or a fresh
search phase turns up more solid matches than remaining budget allows), do
**not** discard the extra candidates and do **not** insert them into
`{{ entity_plural }}` either — that would blow past the review-volume cap
the number exists to protect. Instead:

1. Finish drafting each overflow candidate fully (same as a normal
   candidate — see Step 4) so no research work is wasted.
2. Append each as one JSON line to
   `{{ config_dir_name }}/overflow_backlog.jsonl` (append-only; one
   candidate object per line, same shape as the INSERT in Step 5 minus the
   SQL).
3. Note the overflow count in the run summary and in `scout_runs`.

At the **start** of every run, before doing any fresh searching: read
`{{ config_dir_name }}/overflow_backlog.jsonl` if it's non-empty, and
insert backlog entries (oldest first) as `{{ queued_stage }}` rows up to
that run's remaining budget ({{ scout_candidates_cap }}, or
{{ scout_candidates_target }} if you want to leave headroom for fresh finds
— use judgment). Remove drained entries from the file by rewriting it
without them. Only do fresh searching once the backlog is empty or the cap
is hit by backlog entries alone.

### On hitting the hard cap

- Send a push notification if the `PushNotification` tool is available in
  this session (it usually is on a scheduled run) — you should know the
  supply is exceeding the review cap without having to check.
- Always state it plainly in the final chat summary regardless.
- Do not treat a capped run as a problem to fix by raising the cap
  yourself — that's your call to make outside this skill. Just report it.

## Inputs (read fresh every run)

- `{{ config_dir_name }}/criteria.md` — the constraints and dealbreakers that
  define a good match. If this file still has unfilled placeholders, proceed
  but be conservative about what counts as a match, and mention in the
  summary that criteria.md needs filling in.
- `{{ config_dir_name }}/search_terms.md` — the search terms{% if uses_web %} and site list{% endif %}
  to use. Placeholder lines like "_(term 1)_" mean it hasn't been filled in:
  say so in the summary and fall back to what `criteria.md` and the profile imply.
{% if uses_web %}
- `{{ config_dir_name }}/rotation_state.json` — `{term_index, source_index}`.
  Used only for web-search terms: read it, compute this run's slice (see
  Budget table, unless it's "all"), and at the end of the run write back the
  new indices (advance by however many were covered, wrapping around each
  list's length).
{% endif %}
{% if uses_boards %}
- `{{ config_dir_name }}/watch_companies.txt` — companies to check directly
  every run (one per line, `#` lines ignored).
- `{{ config_dir_name }}/known_boards.json` — cache of companies whose job
  board has been found (or looked for and not found). The tools maintain it;
  don't hand-edit unless a hit is wrong.
{% endif %}
{% if artifact_enabled %}
- A profile/background doc — check `{{ config_dir_name }}/profile.md`
  first, then any PDF/doc file already sitting in that folder before
  assuming there isn't one (people often drop in a resume under its own
  name rather than renaming it). Only stop and tell the user to add one if
  the folder genuinely has nothing usable.
{% endif %}
- `{{ config_dir_name }}/reviewed_postings.jsonl`, if present — every
  listing/posting URL any past run has actually fetched and evaluated
  (candidate or not). Skip fetching any URL already in this file rather
  than re-reviewing it — cheaper than even the dedup-set check, since it
  avoids the fetch entirely, not just the insert. The helper tools already
  hide these.

## Step 1 — drain the backlog{% if artifact_enabled %} and fulfill draft requests{% endif %}


See "Overflow backlog" above. Do this before any fresh searching.
{% if artifact_enabled %}

Also check for explicit requests first — you can click "Request
{{ artifact_label }}" on any {{ queued_stage | tojson }} row from the
dashboard, which sets `{{ artifact_field_prefix }}_requested = true`:

```sql
SELECT id, {{ primary_field }}, {{ secondary_field }}, source_link FROM {{ entity_plural }}
WHERE current_stage = {{ queued_stage | sqlstr }} AND {{ artifact_field_prefix }}_requested = true AND {{ artifact_field_prefix }}_draft IS NULL;
```

Draft these via the {{ artifact_slug }} skill regardless of `match_tier` or
draft scope — these exist because the automatic
`{{ artifact_field_prefix }}_needed` detection missed one you actually want.
This doesn't count against the run's search budget; it's not new research.
{% endif %}

## Step 2 — build the dedup set

Query the tracker so nothing gets suggested twice:

```sql
SELECT {{ primary_field }}, {{ secondary_field }}, source_link FROM {{ entity_plural }};
```

Skip any candidate that already has *any* row (any stage, all of it) unless
it's a genuinely different, clearly distinct {{ entity_singular }} from the
same source (use judgment — don't re-suggest the same thing under a
slightly different title). Also skip anything already sitting in
`overflow_backlog.jsonl`.

**Cooldown for stale sources.** Scan `reviewed_postings.jsonl` for companies
(or sources) with **2 or more `"rejected"` entries whose reason says
stale/closed/404/expired within the last 30 days**. Treat those as cooling
down for this run: if one turns up again, don't fetch it — log it as
`verdict: "skipped"` with a `reason` noting the cooldown. Two hits, not one,
is the threshold (a single stale hit is often just cache lag). The cooldown
expires on its own 30 days after the most recent hit, so recompute it from the
log each run rather than keeping a separate list.

## Step 3 — search (within budget)

{{ scout_subagents_per_run }} subagent(s), not a fan-out, cover every enabled
source below. Fetch a posting's full text only for candidates that look like
plausible matches from the title/snippet first (don't fetch everything
indiscriminately — that's what burns the fetch budget fast), skip anything
already in `reviewed_postings.jsonl`, filter against the dedup set and
`criteria.md`, and **stop as soon as the early-exit trigger fires**
({{ scout_candidates_target }} A-tier candidates) — don't keep going through the
rest of the sources just because they're there.
{% if job_sources %}

### The helper tools

Run these from the project root with `python3`, **always written out in full
exactly as in the examples below** (`python3 {{ tools }}/<tool>.py ...`). Don't
put the path in a shell variable or build the command in a loop: permission
rules match the command as written, so a variable gets it blocked, both in
the unattended daily run and as extra prompts for the person. Likewise run
database commands as `psql -d {{ db_name }} ...`. Each prints **one JSON object
per line**; notes and errors go to stderr. They already hide postings in
`reviewed_postings.jsonl` and companies that already have a tracker row
(add `--all` to see them anyway). If a tool exits non-zero, say which source
was unavailable in the summary and carry on with the others — don't retry in
a loop.

Every tool takes the same filters — build them from `search_terms.md`,
`criteria.md` and the profile, not from guesses:

- `--include "staff,senior backend"` — keep titles matching ANY item (all
  words in an item must appear).
- `--exclude "intern,manager"` — drop titles matching ANY item.
- `--location "remote,new york"` — keep locations matching ANY item;
  `remote` matches roles that are actually remote.
- `--limit 40` — cap how many come back (default 100).

The tools are only fetchers and keyword filters. **You** decide fit: read the
posting against `criteria.md` before anything becomes a candidate.

**`posting_link` must be the specific posting's own URL** (the `url` field),
never a company's general careers page or a search-results page.
{% endif %}
{% if uses_boards %}

### Company job boards (Greenhouse, Ashby)

Many companies publish open roles through Greenhouse or Ashby, both of which
have public APIs that need no login. This is the cheapest, most reliable
source: use the live board, not a cached search snippet.

```
python3 {{ tools }}/boards.py sweep --include "..." --exclude "..." --location "..."
python3 {{ tools }}/boards.py jobs "Company Name" --include "..."
python3 {{ tools }}/boards.py lookup "Company Name" ["Another Co" ...]
python3 {{ tools }}/boards.py describe --ats greenhouse --slug SLUG --id ID
```

1. **`sweep`** checks every company already in `known_boards.json` plus every
   name in `watch_companies.txt`, in one pass. Start here.
2. **`lookup "Company"`** finds a company's board from its name and caches the
   result. It tries a handful of likely names on each service, and **never
   guesses silently**: a Greenhouse hit is only accepted if the board's own
   name matches, and an Ashby hit is marked `"verified": false` because Ashby
   returns no company name — open one of its job URLs and confirm it really is
   that company before relying on it. A miss lists exactly which names were
   tried and is remembered for 30 days (`--refresh` to retry).
3. **`jobs "Company"`** lists one company's open roles, filtered.
4. **`describe`** prints one posting's full text, using the `ref` from a
   result (`--ats`, `--slug`, `--id`). It counts against the full-fetch cap.
   If it exits 3 the posting was taken down: log it as rejected (stale).

Where do new company names come from? From the user (`watch_companies.txt`),
from companies named in `criteria.md` or the profile{% if uses_web %}, and from web search{% endif %}.
Don't invent companies. Add a company to `watch_companies.txt` only if the
user asks.
{% endif %}
{% if uses_yc %}

### Y Combinator startup jobs

```
python3 {{ tools }}/yc.py --role software-engineer --locations remote,new-york --include "..." --location "..."
```

Reads YC's public listing pages. `--role` takes one or more of
`software-engineer, designer, product-manager, recruiting-hr, sales-manager,
marketing, support, operations, science`; `--locations` takes
`remote, san-francisco, new-york, los-angeles, seattle, austin, chicago,
india`. Each page only lists the ~40 newest postings, so combine the roles and
locations that fit `criteria.md` to widen coverage. Internships are skipped
unless you pass `--internships`. If YC changes its page format the tool says
so and exits 2 — report the source as unavailable that day.
{% endif %}
{% if uses_hn %}

### Hacker News "Who is hiring?"

```
python3 {{ tools }}/hn.py --include "..." --location "..."      # newest monthly thread
python3 {{ tools }}/hn.py --month 2026-08 --include "..."       # an earlier month
```

Each top-level comment in the monthly thread is one posting, written by the
company itself. `--include` / `--exclude` search the whole comment, not just a
title. Results include the `header` line, the first part of the `text`, any
`emails` found, and `"outreach": true` when the comment gives an email address
or invites direct contact. A posting's own `url` is its comment link. Company
and title are best-effort guesses from the comment's first line — read the
`header` yourself. Read `text` (and open the comment link if you need more)
before deciding.
{% endif %}
{% if uses_web %}

### Web search

Use web search for the terms in `search_terms.md`, this run's slice of them
per the Budget table. Search results are often stale: when a result names a
company, check its **live** job board{% if uses_boards %} with `boards.py lookup` /
`jobs`{% endif %} instead of trusting a cached snippet, and skip anything already
in `reviewed_postings.jsonl`. If web search is unavailable at the start of a
run, don't stall — carry on with the other sources.
{% endif %}
{% if scout_outreach_mode %}

### Outreach-signal detection (applies to every source)

Some postings invite a message to a real person instead of a form. This is a
tag, not a bypass: the posting must still clear `criteria.md` normally. Watch
for it while reading a posting you were already going to read:

1. **Direct-outreach language:** "email us at…", "reach out to [name]
   directly", "skip the form", "no recruiter", "we read every application
   ourselves".
2. **A personal or team email address** in the posting (Hacker News results
   set `"outreach": true` and list `emails` for you).
3. **A non-standard posting page** (a custom careers page or plain form) is
   only corroborating evidence alongside #1 or #2, never enough on its own.

When it fires on a genuine candidate:
- Prefix `notes` with `[Direct outreach]` before the usual one-line rationale.
- Append `" (direct outreach)"` to `found_via`.
- Pass the discovered contact (name and/or email) to the
  {{ artifact_slug }} skill so it uses its **Direct outreach mode**.
{% endif %}

### Logging

For each posting actually fetched (whether it became a candidate or not),
append one line to `{{ config_dir_name }}/reviewed_postings.jsonl`: `{url,
{{ primary_field }}, {{ secondary_field }}, verdict, reason, reviewed_at, found_via}`
where `verdict` is `"queued"`, `"backlog"`, `"rejected"`, or `"skipped"`
(cooldown — nothing was fetched). Two rules matter:

- **`url` is required on every `"rejected"` entry** — a rejected entry with
  no URL can never stop a future run from re-fetching the same dead posting.
- A one-line `reason` is required for rejected/skipped entries, and
  `found_via` should name the source or search term, so a later run can see
  which sources are actually productive.

For each surviving candidate, capture: {{ primary_label | lower }}, {{ secondary_label | lower }}, location,
{% if has_value %}{{ value_label | lower }} (if disclosed), {% endif %}{% if has_category %}{{ category_label | lower }}, {% endif %}source URL, a short summary, tier (A or B — see Match
tiers), and{% if artifact_enabled %} **explicit language about whether
{{ artifact_label | lower }} is required, optional, or not mentioned at
all**{% else %} anything else worth flagging{% endif %}.


